//! One description per primitive (spec 079, AC9): what it does, its arguments' meaning
//! and its result's states, for whoever is about to call it. The MCP tool's
//! description and the subcommand's `--help` are both this text, and a command or a
//! reference cites the primitive for its behavior rather than restating it.
//!
//! A change to what a primitive does edits one constant here. `{n}` must not appear
//! in one, since clap's help renderer turns it into a newline: write `<n>`.

#![allow(missing_docs)] // Each constant is named for its primitive, which says all a doc would.

/// What the `ductus` list and `-h` show of a description: up to its first sentence's
/// end, or up to its first colon when that comes sooner, since some first sentences
/// run to a thousand bytes by listing what follows the colon.
#[must_use]
pub fn summary(description: &'static str) -> &'static str {
    let sentence = description.find(". ").map(|end| end + 1);
    let clause = description.find(": ");
    let end = match (sentence, clause) {
        (Some(sentence), Some(clause)) => sentence.min(clause),
        (Some(end), None) | (None, Some(end)) => end,
        (None, None) => description.len(),
    };
    description.get(..end).unwrap_or(description)
}

pub const APPEND_INBOX: &str = "Append one `- [ ] {text}` checkbox bullet to {specs-root}/inbox.md (atomic write), creating the file when missing. The surface behind /{project}:log, the inbox's only producer. Returns item-count, the total inbox bullets (comment/fence-aware) after the call.";
pub const APPEND_QUESTION: &str = "Append one `- {question}` bullet to the target artifact's ## Open Questions section — /{project}:amend's question-route write. Targets the feature's spec.md, or scenarios/{slug}.md when `scenario` is passed. Dedup is normalized-whitespace + case-insensitive (a match is a clean appended: false outcome reporting the existing entry); a missing section is created per template order; on a spec target whose status is clarified/planned/in-progress the status reverts to draft in the same atomic write (the back-edge). A `done` spec is excluded (§spec-lifecycle): the question is appended but status stays done, since done reopens via the scenario route, not a question.";
pub const APPEND_TASK: &str = "Append a numbered task block to a feature's tasks.md (atomic rewrite). It can succeed writing nothing, when a task already references the scenario or a pending one has the same title and items, so read its result, not its exit status. Its `task-number` is a string, the number of the task written or matched: a matched task may carry a hand-inserted suffix (`11a`). A new task is numbered past the highest digits and never takes a suffix. A tasks.md carrying a `next-task:` frontmatter counter (written by prune-tasks, so a pruned number is never reissued) takes the larger of the counter and the highest digits present plus one, then advances the counter; a counter at or below the highest present never causes a duplicate, one that is not a positive integer is an error, and a file with no counter is numbered from the highest present and gains none.";
pub const APPLY_MANIFEST: &str = "Strategy-aware bulk substitute + write driven by a manifest. An entry setting tool-search-line, as Claude Code's command copies do (spec 080), ends its `## Instructions` preamble after substitution with a line that loads, in one ToolSearch that asks for the command's first ductus call to go in the same message, the tools its steps name and each tool the command file or a section it cites invokes (a `§` or bold heading citation, followed transitively, counting only a name written as `invoke` then the backticked name, outside fenced blocks and HTML comments), less those named by the commands/target.md the manifest itself installs, in either source layout, which load with the session. When that entry is pinned, its installed copy at the destination is read instead, as the MCP server reads it, and one missing or unparseable leaves out none. A reference it names as .ductus/reference/<name>.md is read from reference/ beside that commands/ directory. No line is written when none remain. The call halts before any write when the manifest installs no commands/target.md, installs two different ones, or installs an unpinned one that is missing or does not parse. A reference a command names that is not in the source root halts it before any write for a command or prompt destination, and otherwise when the walk reaches the entry. With a non-empty substitutions map it refuses, before any write, an entry whose text would still carry one of the four placeholders the installer declares ({project}, {cli-config-dir}, {project-name}, {One-line project description.}) that the map lacks and the entry does not keep literal, naming the entry and the placeholder; an empty map substitutes nothing and is never refused (spec 022).";
pub const BRIEF_RE_READ: &str = "Open a /{project}:review fix round (spec 078). It snapshots the working tree, and the files inside the project that differ between `base` and that snapshot, tree to tree, the feature's own review.md left out, are divided in three. `excluded` holds what only commits another spec claims changed, with no uncommitted edit, of content or mode, on top at either snapshot (spec 079). `exempt` holds each analyze subject of the feature that is not a durable contract (spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md and the like, which /{project}:analyze reads again before done, but for a criterion's tick, outside the analysis digest). `paths` holds the rest, all the brief names. `kind` is `re-read` when `paths` is not empty, `empty` when all three are, and `exempt` otherwise. `exempt` and `re-read` return that snapshot as `snapshot`, the next round's base. A `re-read` also writes `round.toml` and the re-read's whole brief under .ductus/review-rounds/{feature}/{base}/ — the paths, the diff command, the severity bar and the result-file contract, nothing from the caller — and names the `result` file the re-read writes. With `[review] re-reads-off` set, `re-reads-off` carries the reason, no snapshot is returned and no brief is written. Briefing a base again replaces its round. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`, `paths`, `exempt` and `excluded` are absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding them whole, one entry per line; `kind` and paths-count stay inline in both forms. An error is never an empty round.";
pub const CHECK_ARTIFACT_SIZE: &str = "Measure a feature's spec artifacts (spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, data-model.md, and each scenario — never research.md, review.md or analysis.md) by their length on disk against the configured read size, `[artifacts] read-size-bytes` in .ductus/config.toml (default 50000 when unset; a value that is not a positive whole number, or a non-table `artifacts`, is an error naming the setting, the value and the file, never the default). Each artifact over it is reported with its size, read-page count, the fixes for its kind and, on a done spec, whether each reopens it, a warning saying it may not be read in one call, the artifact-size finding message /{project}:analyze records, and whether a stored waiver in analysis.md still covers it (a waiver of the same path at a read-page count at least this one's), with that waiver's key. A missing artifact is not reported; one that cannot be opened is listed in `skipped`, never as clean. A key in `[artifacts]` other than read-size-bytes is named in `notices`. With `project-files`, also measure the four project files (AGENTS.md at the project root, and system.md, events.md and errors.md under the spec root) against the same threshold; `feature` is then optional, and a call naming neither `feature` nor `project-files` is an argument error. The result keeps `threshold`, `examined`, `oversized`, `skipped` and `notices` at the top level, then two halves that share no key: the spec's (`feature`, `status`, `decisions`, `path`) and `project-files`, one record per file with its state (measured, absent, unreadable) and bytes. A project file over the threshold joins `oversized` as kind `project-file`, whose one fix is /{project}:prune --project, with no on-done; unlike a spec subject a missing project file is reported, as state `absent`, and one that cannot be read is in `skipped`. With a feature, a stored waiver in its analysis.md covers a project file as it covers a spec subject. With `staged` (requires `project-files`, excludes `feature`), measure each file's index copy against its HEAD copy, adding `head-bytes` and `refused` (the staged copy is over the threshold and has no HEAD copy, or is larger than it); a file absent from the index, a deleted one included, is absent and never refused; the CLI exits 1 when any file is refused or could not be measured and names each on stderr, and a repository that cannot be read is an error. Read-only. Used by /{project}:clarify, /{project}:plan, /{project}:analyze (spec 063) and the shipped pre-commit hook's staged check (spec 075). With `staged`, each refused or unmeasurable file is named on stderr with its size, the limit and /{project}:prune --project.";
pub const CHECK_ARTIFACTS: &str = "Run /{project}:analyze's residual deterministic check families against one feature: artifact completeness per status tier (plan.md/tasks.md at planned+), task consistency (numbering strictly increasing by digits, then suffix: `11` < `11a` < `12`; Done-when presence), scenario→task mapping (a scenario any revision of tasks.md named is satisfied, so a §tasks-phase pruned task never counts against it), review-state drift on done specs, an unset last-run, blocking: true, an outstanding SHOULD, a broken contract in open-findings, an open fix-round mark, an unticked criterion and an unchecked box in tasks.md among it, disposition drift on done specs (advisory: review.md counting undispositioned findings, of which only a broken contract holds done; a record predating the dispositions map is silent), cross-spec impact drift on done specs (advisory: a cross-spec-impact entry no back-link discharges), scenario open questions (blocking at done, advisory otherwise), link-adjacent decision drift (prose asserting an open state its own sibling link's target contradicts), acceptance-criterion path existence on in-progress and done specs (a path the repository's own ignore rules ignore is skipped as gitignored, not flagged), body path existence on in-progress and done specs (a path named in the body, plan, data model or scenarios that existed in the repository and no longer resolves), and acceptance-criterion labels (duplicate AC<n>, a next-criterion that no longer exceeds the body, an unlabelled criterion in a labelled spec), spec integrity (blocking: no Acceptance Criteria section, no criterion at clarified or later, an empty or placeholder criterion, an open question in the body at clarified or later) and plan consistency (blocking when plan.md exists: no link to or name of its spec, no decision under Technical Decisions, no path under Affected Files). Each finding carries family, severity, message, and path; severity is blocking for artifact completeness, task consistency, spec integrity, plan consistency and review-state drift, and at done for scenario open questions and an artifact it could not read, and advisory for the rest. Targets a family could not examine are reported separately in `skipped`, so a zero-finding result never reads as assurance the primitive cannot give, a file in the feature directory that links outside the project among them (`target-unparseable`, family `outward-link`, never opened); at any other status each of the two path families skips the spec's spec.md once as status-gated. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: findings and skipped are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const CHECK_COMMAND_FLAGS: &str = "Report flags a command's Flags table documents but its `argument-hint:` frontmatter omits. `argument-hint` is the surface a host renders when it offers a command, so an omitted flag is one the operator is never shown \u{2014} the defect an adopter hit when review.md's table listed eight flags and its hint named three. Maintainer scope: the subject is framework/commands/, where the divergence originates, not the generated copies an adopter cannot repair. Only a `Flags` section's table rows count (a command documenting flags in prose is examined and contributes nothing), and only each row's first cell, so behavior prose naming another flag is not a finding; fenced example tables are excluded. Empty `findings` means examined-and-clean over `with-flags-table`, not over `examined`; an unreadable file lands in `skipped`, and `guidance` is set when no Flags table was found at all, which is an extraction failure rather than a clean corpus.";
pub const CHECK_CORPUS_LINKS: &str = "Report relative markdown links in the spec corpus that resolve to nothing \u{2014} the failure an adopter creates by deleting or renaming a spec directory, which dangles every inbound pointer with no error and no gate. Fills the gap between three checks that each miss it: check-orphaned-references scopes to adopter-owned bootstrap referrers, /{project}:analyze is bounded to one feature plus its dependencies, and /{project}:audit Family 26 is maintainer-only. Read-only but for the list file past the read size, and reports rather than repairs \u{2014} a depth error is re-pointed while a deliberate removal is named in prose, and a rewrite that guessed wrong is worse than a precise report. Resolution is lexical against the citing file's own directory, never canonicalized, so the answer is a property of the text rather than of the checkout. Inline code spans are stripped before matching (documentation quoting link syntax is not making a link), fenced blocks and frontmatter are skipped, and blockquoted lines are NOT \u{2014} that exemption belongs to derive-dependencies, where it decides whether a link induces an edge. Empty `broken` means clean only when `skipped` is empty and `examined` is non-empty; `guidance` is set when the scan could not establish a subject at all. Targets carrying NNN, a {placeholder}, a `*`, or a bare `...` are counted in `shapes-skipped` rather than tested, and the spec root's templates/ are excluded by construction and counted. With `scope: repository` the subject is every tracked .md file, and every agent's generated command copies, framework/templates/project/, runtime/tests/ and \u{2014} where framework/reference/ holds their sources \u{2014} the generated .ductus/reference/ copies are excluded by construction and counted too. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: broken, examined and skipped are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole. The CLI reports every broken link on stderr from the whole scan, and its exit status reads the counts.";
pub const CHECK_ORPHANED_REFERENCES: &str = "Report adopter-owned files (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, README.md, .githooks/pre-commit, and the spec root's system.md) whose references to ductus-managed paths no longer resolve — the failure a migration chain leaves behind when a later entry moves a path an earlier entry wrote into a create-strategy file the manifest never overwrites. Read-only, and reports rather than repairs: the adopter may have hand-edited the reference. Attribution has two modes and the result names which ran — `registry` when framework/migrations.toml is readable in the project tree (a framework checkout, where it names the entry whose target_paths covers the missing path), `watermark` otherwise (an adopter checkout, where only .ductus/config.toml's [migrations].last_applied is available and `migration` is empty). An unreadable referrer lands in `skipped`, so empty `findings` means examined-and-clean only when `skipped` is empty; `examined` names the files the result actually describes.";
pub const CHECK_PRESCRIPTIONS: &str = "The diff check of a /{project}:review fix round (spec 078). `base` names the PREVIOUS round: its round.toml gives the snapshot that re-read read, and its result.json the edits it prescribed. Each prescribed file is taken from that snapshot, its edits applied in order — each `old` replaced only where it occurs at exactly one position, overlapping ones counted — and compared, as git stores both, content and mode, with a fresh snapshot of the working tree, returned as `snapshot` on a match alone, as the next round's base. A file matches with every edit applied, or with only its MUST and SHOULD findings' edits, so its NOTE edits may be declined, all of them or none. `failed` lists, for a file that matches neither, each edit whose `old` occurred zero times or more than once; `differing` the prescribed files that match neither; `unprescribed` the files changed since the re-read's snapshot that no edit names, the feature's own review.md and, as brief-re-read sets them apart, what only commits another spec claims changed left out (spec 079). A MUST or SHOULD on a path outside the round counts as a NOTE. `verdict` is `matched` when all three are empty, `differs` otherwise, and `could-not-run` — never a match — when no round was briefed at the base, the result file is missing, incomplete or unparseable, it prescribes no edit, no round.toml was written at the base or it does not parse, or an edit names a path outside the project, with `problem` saying which. `open` counts the re-read's MUST and SHOULD findings with no edits: a match ends the loop only when it is zero. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`, the three lists are absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding them whole, pretty-printed; `verdict` and every count stay inline in both forms. Writes git objects and, past the read size, that file, .ductus/results/.gitignore and the removal of the feature's stale list files, only.";
pub const CHECK_PROMOTION_COVERAGE: &str = "How much of a rules file is routed to its canonical source: rule-bearing minus (table-keyed union pointer-citing). A notice, never a gate.";
pub const CHECK_REVIEW_GATE: &str = "Evaluate /{project}:implement's pre-done review gate for one feature, in order: whether the spec is already done, the feature directory's markdown lint (via the lint-markdown machinery), unresolved scenario open questions, an unchecked box in tasks.md, under any heading, an undischarged cross-spec impact, the review record in review.md, whether review.md predates finding dispositions (no dispositions: map), whether an outstanding gating finding remains in it, in practice a SHOULD neither waived nor released by its deferral, naming each deferral that holds it with its work reference (spec 076), whether it still carries fix-rounds-open, its loop cut off between its first and last write (spec 079), or a fix round that needed a re-read has neither one nor a waiver in round-waivers (spec 078), and whether it lists open-findings or compute-review-scope reads review-owed, naming what changed or was left open, a review clearing it once its record is committed (spec 079); then, since review.md is one of an analysis's subjects, the analyze record in analysis.md, with a decisions: list that does not parse reading as unreadable, whether that analysis is still current, whether it names a loaded rule in unjudged-rules, which no run has judged (spec 082), whether it predates finding dispositions and whether an outstanding gating finding remains in it. Undispositioned advisory findings and observations never block (spec 058), a broken contract left open apart (spec 079). A record without the loop fields passes with guidance that says so, as does a window that could not be read. Neither record lives in spec.md frontmatter (spec 057). A declared folds-into is not checked: a pending fold never holds done, and the fold is run after it (spec 051). Returns the verdict plus, when blocked, the first failing check and the canonical blocked message; a blocked gate is a domain outcome the host halts on, never an error. On an analyze-stale block the result also lists every changed artifact in analyze-stale-paths, of which the message shows three. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: violations, cross-spec-impact and analyze-stale-paths are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const READ_DEPRECATION_LABELS: &str = "Read the deprecation labels of rule files, for the release audit's Family 40 (spec 067). Returns one entry for each line of a given rule file containing `**DEPRECATED`, by file and 1-based line: `rules` lists the rules it labels, those whose section it is the first label in, as `check-rule-ids` reads a rule deprecated (the one shared reader); `follows` lists each rule in whose section it follows an earlier label, with that label's line; a line in no rule section has neither list. A labelled rule's label gives its `deprecated-in` and `removal-target` when well formed, as MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, the form `**DEPRECATED in X.Y.Z:**` opening its line, naming the target once, in any case, as `removed in X.Y.Z`, in the paragraph that ends at a blank line, a heading, a block quote, a `**Field:**` line, a list item or a fence (specs/008-security-rules/data-model.md, Deprecated field); otherwise `malformed` gives the reason and the fix. A malformed label is a result, never an error, and hides no other. A rule file that cannot be read or decoded is listed in `unreadable` and not counted in `examined`, and the others are still read. A path that is absolute, climbs out with .. or leaves the project through a symlink is an error before any file is read. Compares nothing with a version: the caller does. Read-only. A rule's section runs from its ID heading to the next heading at its level or above; its label is the first line in it containing `**DEPRECATED`, and a label under a nested rule heading labels both rules. A `removed in` not followed by a version is prose, never a target.";
pub const CHECK_RULE_IDS: &str = "Verify cited rule IDs exist in rule files and aren't deprecated. A `spec.md` whose frontmatter lists removed rules under `retired-rules:` records their retirement: a citation of a listed ID that no rule file defines is reported `retired`, neither found nor missing, and a citation in any other file, or of an unlisted ID, stays missing. A citation no given rule file defines that the framework ships (an index compiled into the runtime), as when a surface's file was never installed, is reported `not-installed`, informational and never missing.";
pub const CHECK_STEP_REFERENCES: &str = "Report `step N` prose references in a command file that name a numbered step the file does not have. A command numbers its Instructions steps and refers to them in prose; the numbers are the only binding, and removing a step renumbers everything after it while the prose keeps the old numbers. Three findings, because the repairs differ: `unresolved` (names a number the file lacks), `self-reference` (a step referring to its own number \u{2014} it resolves, so an existence check passes it, and it is almost always renumbering residue), and `discontinuous` (steps not running 1..n, which makes every reference after the gap ambiguous rather than wrong). Subject is the Instructions section alone: a numbered list elsewhere in the file restarts at 1 and is a different list, so `references-out-of-subject` counts those mentions rather than resolving them (a command's reference, whose sub-procedures restart the same way, is its own file under framework/reference/ and outside the subject), and an empty `findings` is never read as every reference in the file resolving. A qualified reference (groom\u{2019}s step 3) names another file\u{2019}s procedure and is excluded by construction. A file with an Instructions section from which no steps are extracted is a finding, not a pass \u{2014} two empty sets compare equal.";
pub const CHECK_STUCK: &str = "Count `tasks.md` commits since the spec's reopening started. stuck is true when that count reaches threshold and the first unchecked box in `tasks.md` sits on the same line at HEAD as it did at that start, so ticking subtasks without adding any clears it, and a file with no unchecked box at either end, or a spec with no start, never reads stuck. A spec is found through history by its number, so a slug change moves neither the count nor the first box, and a commit with no readable status keeps the start. Returns the count, the start's sha and the threshold, and guidance when a shallow clone cannot place that start, so stuck: false is no answer there.";
pub const CHECK_UNFOLDED_SPECS: &str = "Report branch-scoped specs (`<identifier>.<n>-<slug>`) still present in the working tree, each with the upstream spec its `folds-into:` names and its current status. A branch-scoped directory is a staging form discharged by fold-back; nothing runs on the merge itself, so a merged branch leaves its staging directories behind silently — this is what notices. Read-only but for the list file past the read size, and it reports rather than repairs: which upstream section the content belongs in is the operator's judgement at fold-back. A `folds-into` naming a spec absent from this tree is reported as declared, never as broken — that absence is the normal pre-merge state, and existence is enforced at fold-back by retire-feature. Empty `unfolded` means examined-and-clean only when `examined` is non-zero. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: unfolded is absent, unfolded-count stays inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const CLASSIFY_SPEC_EDIT: &str = "Classify the edit under one feature's directory, from the diff between base (a ref, HEAD by default) and the working tree, untracked files included. Returns kind and reasons: none when nothing but a run's records differs; correction, which leaves a done spec done (§spec-lifecycle), when it changes no acceptance criterion, open question or unchecked task and touches only spec.md, plan.md and tasks.md; mechanical, which leaves it done too, when each edit beyond that, to a scenario, the data model or any other file under the spec, only adds, removes or rewrites a cross-service reference or is a sweep's, by `mechanical_sweep`'s rule, which the analysis record's freshness also uses, every rewritten token pair in more than one changed file, so a real change made with the same token in two files reads as one; reopens otherwise, with reasons from criterion, open-question, unchecked-task, scenario, data-model and other-file. A criterion compares by its text with its AC label and any cross-service reference removed, so assigning a label or a tick is a correction and taking a tick back reopens; a task edit adding no unchecked line, compared by its text under any heading, is a correction, so moving a box is one; a deleted plan.md or tasks.md, or a spec.md absent on either side, is other-file. An edit it could not read is an error, never a correction, as is a `[services]` registry that does not parse or names an entry with no `repo` or `path`, since without it no link can be told to be a reference. Read-only but for a snapshot commit object when such an edit is more than a reference's, which moves no ref (spec 079).";
pub const COMPUTE_REVIEW_SCOPE: &str = "Resolve /{project}:review's diff-base (the later of the parent of the commit its reopening started at, where it last left done or, never done, first entered in-progress, a trip back through draft or a commit with no readable status staying in it, and the last review commit since then that recorded no skipped pass or budget stop and whose record the gate does not hold until a review runs, a commit moving a run's record out of spec.md, a merge carrying one in, or a rename moving it, being none, a commit restoring an older record starting the window at the run it restores, and a restored run the reopening carried in, or a record reverted away, leaving it at the reopen; HEAD while that move is uncommitted, empty when it never happened, or a --since override, which moves only the start), where it came from (base-source), its file scope, and whether a review is owed. A commit belongs to each spec whose directory it touches or whose number its subject scope names. The scope holds the paths of a commit that belongs to the spec alone or to no spec; of a commit whose subject names this spec, every path outside the other claimants' directories, and of one naming other specs only those in this spec's directory (spec 081); of a commit several specs claim that names none, those in the spec's directory or its plan's Affected Files, and a path in none of their plans outside the other specs' directories; uncommitted work; and, wherever the window starts, each path the record at HEAD lists in open-findings and each contract its digest says changed, unless it is gone or leaves the project. A branch-scoped spec removed below done, by a commit in the window or in the working tree, whose folds-into names this spec brings the paths of every commit it claimed, by touching its directory under any slug or naming it, wherever the window starts, unless in a spec's directory or gone, since no review read them; one that reached done had them read by its own review, so its commits stay its own. A plan entry unchanged in the window stays only on the spec's first review. What the scope leaves out is in excluded, and the files deleted since the base are in deleted. review-owed is false when a review record names a run and holds nothing only a review clears (a gating count, no dispositions map, the open fix-round mark, an unwaived round or a finding left open), and the scope and the deletions it attributes to the spec hold no code: every path outside the project's artifacts, which are the spec root, .ductus/, AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md (spec 081). A changed scenario or data model, this spec's or another's, owes an analysis and no review; it only joins the scope of a review that runs. When the result would pass [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default), form is `file`, the scope, modified-since, plan-affected, deleted and excluded lists are absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding all five whole, one entry per line, to read in parts by line offset when it exceeds one read. Each list's count is inline in both forms; a zero scope-count, never an absent list, is an empty scope. A spec is found through history by its number, so a slug changed mid-reopening keeps its window. A shallow clone whose history stops inside the reopening, or short of a fold's commits, is an error rather than a window.";
pub const CREATE_FEATURE: &str = "Scaffold the next {specs-root}/{NNN-slug}/ feature directory: compute the next feature number (max existing prefix + 1, zero-padded), derive the kebab-case slug from the title, create the directory, and copy the spec template into it (atomic, mode-preserving). Refuses with created: false when the target directory already exists.";
pub const CREATE_PLAN_ARTIFACTS: &str = "Copy the plan/tasks (and, with include-data-model, data-model) templates into an existing feature directory (atomic, mode-preserving) — /{project}:plan's template-copy and existing-artifact-detection step. Pre-existing artifacts are never touched by default: each is reported kept (file, path, action, template — primitive results carry no wall-clock data, so the keep-or-replace prompt stats the file itself for any timestamp); only overwrite: true (the confirmed replace branch) copies fresh templates over them.";
pub const CREATE_SCENARIO: &str = "Write a new scenarios/{slug}.md file under a feature with frontmatter and prose body. It writes the frontmatter and the Open and Resolved Questions scaffolding itself, so pass it the body only.";
pub const DASHBOARD: &str = "Single-call pipeline-state data, the structured payload /{project}:status's view is rendered from by render-status, and that /{project}:review --all and /{project}:target read. Returns the per-spec inventory (status, deps, tags, open-question count, artifact existence, scenarios count, blocked-by), the repo-wide tags-union, the config summary (.ductus/config.toml: disabled rule files, and every key in a ductus-owned section that no reader recognizes), the standing inbox (outstanding count and oldest item's date, rendered as an Inbox: line on every run), the reopenings block (measure-reopenings' counts over its default window, rendered as a Reopenings: line on every run, with state not-measured and a reason when the measurement failed), and the optional session target resolved for this process (spec 062): its own target when it has a session identity (DUCTUS_SESSION or a platform session id), else the shared default (.ductus/session.toml, falling back to .govern/session.toml then the legacy root pre-migration). An identified process also gets session-identity, session-source, session-notices and session-unreadable, the session files the peek could not parse. The read is a peek: it pins no adoption and consumes no notice, so the notices shown are the ones the process's next command delivers. The pipeline view itself is render-status's, rendered from the same read (spec 080). Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: specs and tags-union are absent, each with its -count inline, as are session-notices and session-unreadable when non-empty, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole. Each spec also carries `scenario-open-question-count` and `scenarios-with-questions` (the scenarios holding those questions, never summed into `open-question-count`; spec 046), and `folds-into` with `fold-target-missing` (spec 051), a report that the named upstream spec is absent from this corpus, never a verdict.";
pub const RENDER_STATUS: &str = "The pipeline view /{project}:status shows, without dashboard's structured payload (spec 080), rendered from the same read dashboard makes. Takes no arguments. rendered-markdown is the view as one markdown fragment: the target block (the session target this process resolves, its scenario, and each session notice and unreadable session file), the table of every spec, the summary one item per line (counts, blocked, recovery, each unresolved scenario question beside its spec, folds, tags, disabled rule files, unknown config keys, the Reopenings line and the Inbox line), the cross-service references readout, the specs with work left grouped by next action, and the /{project}:target prompt. The whole result is held to [artifacts] read-size-bytes (default 50000). Past it form is `file`: lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ whose `view` holds the whole view's lines, one per entry, and the view is cut, each line standing for a cut naming that file. The done rows collapse to a count line, the rows past the bound are cut and counted, then with no row left the next-action names, and then each other list collapses to its count. project is the slash-command namespace the view's hints name. Read-only but for that file: the session read is a peek, as dashboard's is. A read size that is not a positive whole number is an error, as for every reader of the setting.";
pub const READ_SECTION: &str = "Each named section of a markdown document, in the order asked (spec 080): from its heading line, matched by exact text, to the next heading of the same or a higher level, outside fenced blocks and HTML comments. not-found lists the headings the document lacks; a path leaving the project is refused. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: sections is absent, sections-count stays inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";

pub const RENDER_HELP: &str = "The guide /{project}:help prints (spec 080), so a typed help can be answered with no model turn. Takes no arguments. It reads the installed help copy, the first of {cli-config-dir}/commands/{project}/help.md and {cli-config-dir}/prompts/{project}-help.md that exists, under the agent config directory the session file records (.claude by default). guide is the text between the first line after the copy's ## Instructions heading that is exactly --- and the last such line, with each line that is wholly an HTML comment removed: markdown, with the project's name in each command as the copy spells it. project is the slash-command namespace, which Pi's bridge names its commands from, read without any spec. Read-only. No installed copy is an error naming the paths tried, and a copy with no such heading, fewer than two such lines, or no text between them is an error naming the copy.";
pub const DERIVE_BOUNDARY: &str = "Derive the runtime write boundary from git history. It holds the spec directory's own glob and, for each path changed between that directory's first commit and HEAD, the path's parent directory as a <dir>/** glob, so a new file in a zone the feature touched is admitted; a root-level file stays an exact path, since its zone would admit everything, and a path outside the project is left out. A spec directory no commit touches, or a repository with no commit, is a domain outcome: the boundary is the spec directory's glob alone, with guidance saying so. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: boundary is absent, boundary-count stays inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const DERIVE_DEPENDENCIES: &str = "Regenerate every tracked spec's frontmatter `dependencies:` from the sibling-spec inline links in its body, then report any cycle in the derived graph. Body links are authoritative; the frontmatter is a derived index (spec 017). Links inside frontmatter, fenced code, blockquote-prefixed lines, or a `## See also` section do not induce edges; `## References` does. A spec with no sibling links gets `dependencies: []` — the key present and empty, which is deliberately unlike `derive-references`' absent-when-empty rule. Self-links are recorded rather than stripped so the cycle check surfaces them. Without `write` it reports without writing and pairs with `drift` for a CI check; `staged` limits the rewrite to specs in the pending commit while the cycle check still spans the full graph. Untracked specs are never enumerated and are reported in `untracked-skipped`, so an empty `updated` is not a claim that they are in sync. A spec tracked in the index but missing from the worktree is reported in `absent` and excluded from `examined`, so the count never overstates what was read. A spec whose frontmatter opens a block it never closes, so nothing could be derived, is reported in `unparseable`: an unknown, not drift, which `validate-frontmatter` owns diagnosing. A multi-line flow value (`dependencies: [`, entries, `]` at column zero) is rewritten whole; a rewrite that would still leave a block that does not parse as YAML, a block invalid before it included, is never written and is reported in `unwritable` (absent when empty, with `unwritable-count` inline beside it, absent at zero), whether or not `staged` holds it, since the index is stale and only an edit by hand fixes it. A cycle is a domain outcome in `cycles`, not an error — the caller decides whether it blocks. A malformed [artifacts] read-size-bytes halts before any write. Past it (50,000 by default) form is `file`: updated, unwritten, untracked-skipped, absent, cycles, unparseable and unwritable are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole. The CLI gates on cycles-count and unwritable-count, report-only and with write alike, exiting non-zero, and reports the cycles, and one line per unwritable spec saying to edit its `dependencies:` by hand, on stderr from the whole derivation.";
pub const DERIVE_REFERENCES: &str = "Regenerate every tracked spec's frontmatter `references:` from the cross-service spec links in its body (spec 030). Strictly distinct from `dependencies:` — a reference is informative navigation and never enters the blocking dependency graph; this primitive never reads or writes that field, and relative sibling links are never references. Matches absolute http(s) links whose path holds `/<root>/NNN-slug/spec(-and-plan).md`, resolving each repo against the `[services]` registry: a registered service with a reachable checkout matches only that checkout's own `[paths] specs-root`, while an unreachable or unregistered repo accepts any single segment and records `service: null` rather than dropping the reference. Applies the shared body exclusions plus one of its own — a backticked link is an illustrative example, not a reference. Absent-when-empty: a spec with no cross-service links carries no `references:` key and a stale block is removed, deliberately unlike `derive-dependencies`' `dependencies: []`. `registered-services` reports the registry size, so 'no references' is distinguishable from 'no registry'. A config that does not parse, or a `[services.<alias>]` entry whose `repo` or `path` is absent or empty, halts naming the file, and the entry's alias and field, rather than reading as no registry, which would write `service: null` into every reference. `staged` filters the *write*, never the walk: a reference derives from the `[services]` registry as well as the body, so a service rename drifts specs nobody edited and therefore nobody staged. Every tracked spec is examined, and drifted-but-unstaged specs are reported in `unwritten` — neither 'in sync' nor 'not examined'. Untracked specs are never enumerated and appear in `untracked-skipped`. A spec tracked in the index but missing from the worktree is reported in `absent` and excluded from `examined`, so the count never overstates what was read. A spec whose frontmatter opens a block it never closes, so nothing could be derived, is reported in `unparseable`: an unknown, not drift, which `validate-frontmatter` owns diagnosing. A multi-line flow value (`references: [`, entries, `]` at column zero) is rewritten whole; a rewrite that would still leave a block that does not parse as YAML, a block invalid before it included, is never written and is reported in `unwritable` (absent when empty), whether or not `staged` holds it, since the index is stale and only an edit by hand fixes it. The CLI exits non-zero when `unwritable` is non-empty, report-only and with write alike, and writes one line per unwritable spec to stderr saying to edit its `references:` by hand.";
pub const DERIVE_ROUTING_CANDIDATES: &str = "Derive the existing homes proposed work could belong to, so /{project}:specify runs the routing decision before create-feature writes anything, and /{project}:groom before it routes an inbox item. Three sources: runtime-work (the description names a primitive or a `runtime/` path, routed to the spec whose plan claims `runtime/` — derived from the corpus, never a hardcoded slug), rule-surface (a rule file whose category stem the description shares), and spec-corpus (a spec whose slug it shares vocabulary with). Each candidate names the route it implies (`rule` or `scenario`), the target's status, and whether accepting it implies the done -> in-progress back-edge. Matching is lexical and advisory — the semantic call stays at the routeInboxItem extension point and the decision stays with the operator. Every source lands in `sources-examined` or in `skipped`, so empty candidates with empty skipped means 'examined and matched nothing' while a non-empty `skipped` means 'could not derive' — different answers. `routed-by` marks a tree that already ran and returns gate-required: false. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: candidates is absent, candidates-count stays inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved list whole.";
pub const DIFF_CROSS_SPEC: &str = "Compute /{project}:implement's cross-spec impact surface: the paths under the spec root, outside the feature's own directory, that differ from the window's start and that a commit belonging to the spec changed or that carry an uncommitted change, so another spec's commit is absent. The window starts at the parent of the commit the spec's reopening started at, where it last left done or, never done, first entered in-progress, HEAD while that move is uncommitted, or for a spec never in-progress its first spec-dir commit, reported as window-base. {specs-root}/inbox.md is excluded: it holds what a person logs, which is no spec's impact. A directory of the spec's own number is the spec under an earlier slug, not a sibling. A zero cross-spec-count is the no-impact outcome, and a result carrying guidance has no window, as with no commit yet or a shallow clone that cannot place the start. When the result would pass [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default), form is `file`, cross-spec-paths is absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the list whole, one entry per line, to read in parts by line offset when it exceeds one read. Read-only but for that file.";
pub const DISCOVER_RULE_FILES: &str = "Select rule files for /{project}:review by suffix, [rules] surfaces, and disabled-rule-files, with the notice lines to emit, among them one per unknown key in [rules] or [review]. `all` selects every rule file in the directory past all three, as /{project}:analyze loads them, and validates neither [rules] surfaces nor the disabled entries, which select nothing then. With `rules` (spec 080) the result also lists each selected file's rules, then each `extra-files` entry's, as rule_sections parses them: ID, heading line, tier (`must` or `should`, absent when the Statement carries neither keyword) and Statement, plus the Verification with `verification`; a file that cannot be read is named under `unreadable`, never dropped. Under `all` each file also lists `other-headings`, every heading that opens no rule with its line and its body, the lines under it up to the next heading, since a heading the rule-ID grammar rejects, a malformed rule heading among them, is no rule to the parser. `verification` or `extra-files` without `rules` is an error. With `all`, `rules` and `feature` (spec 082) the result also carries `verdicts`, read from the feature's analysis.md: `judge`, the IDs of the assessable rules with no current stored verdict, which an analysis judges, and `reusable`, each current verdict that fired (id, tier, and its cite, or its at and message) to report again without judging the rule; a rule in neither is current and did not fire. A verdict is current while the spec's design subjects (spec, plan, data model, scenarios, never tasks.md or review.md) and the rule's file are unchanged and the rule was judged. `reason` says why nothing is reusable: `no-prior-verdicts`, `subjects-changed` or `record-unreadable` (also named in a notice), and `judge` then holds every assessable rule. A feature without `all` and `rules` adds nothing, and one that does not exist is an error. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: rule-files, and with verdicts its reusable and judge lists, are absent, their counts (rule-files-count, and reusable-count and judge-count inside verdicts) stay inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const ENFORCE_MANIFEST: &str =
    "Remove files in a directory that are not in the expected manifest.";
pub const EXTRACT_ARCHIVE: &str =
    "Extract a local `.tar.gz` / `.zip` archive into a destination directory.";
pub const FETCH_ARCHIVE: &str = "Download an archive plus its sha256 sidecar and verify the hash.";
pub const GATE_CONFIRM: &str = "Surface a pipeline gate. As an MCP tool it returns the gate prompt payload and never blocks: the orchestrator routes the prompt to the user and supplies the confirmed decision out-of-band. On the CLI it emits a gate-confirm envelope on stdout and blocks on one gate-response line on stdin.";
pub const INVALIDATE_REVIEW: &str = "Reset a spec's review.md record to the un-reviewed state — `last-run` and `reviewed-against` nulled, counts zeroed, `blocking` cleared, that run's other outcomes removed, its excluded count, broken contracts and open fix-round mark among them, with `open-findings` kept, since the next scope reads it — so the pre-`done` gate demands a fresh review before the spec can complete. The gate owes a review for code alone (spec 081), so a fold-back, whichever artifacts it routes into the upstream spec, makes none owed, and would leave a review that never saw the code the fold brought with it. This is how the fold says what the diff cannot see; what the next review reads of that code is compute-review-scope's. Waivers survive verbatim, adopter-authored extra fields included: an invalidation says the review is out of date, not that an operator's recorded judgement about a finding was withdrawn. `invalidated: false` is the domain outcome for a spec that records no current review — already in this state — so a re-run of an interrupted fold converges rather than halting.";
pub const LABEL_CRITERIA: &str = "Assign stable AC<n> labels to a spec's acceptance criteria and maintain the next-criterion counter (idempotent; already-labelled criteria are left byte-identical).";
pub const LINT_MARKDOWN: &str = "Wrap `npx markdownlint-cli2` and surface violations. Given paths or globs it lints exactly the files they name (the config's globs are not appended) and reports violations only in them; given none it lints what the config's globs name. Paths that name no file are not clean: clean is false with no violations and exit-code 0. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: violations is absent, violations-count stays inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const MARK_CRITERION: &str = "Flip a single acceptance-criterion checkbox in `spec.md`.";
pub const MARK_TASK: &str = "Flip a single subtask checkbox in `tasks.md` (atomic rewrite). A subtask is addressed by task number and 0-based index, in a flat or phased file alike; the number may carry a suffix (`11a`). The result states the previous value, and a flip that changes nothing writes nothing. A checkbox-form Done-when clause sits outside the index and follows the tally both ways: it ticks when the flip leaves every subtask checked and unticks when it leaves any unchecked.";
pub const MEASURE_REOPENINGS: &str = "Measure from the repository's history whether reopenings stayed within the bound on review and analysis runs (spec 079). A reopening is one pass into in-progress and on to done, by the status of each spec.md at each commit, starting where it left done, a trip back through draft staying in it, counted when its closing commit falls in the window: since, a date by that commit's author date or a ref for what it does not reach, the last 30 days when absent, at the ref at, HEAD when absent. A commit dated before the window ends no walk, since a child can be dated older than its parent. A run is a distinct last-run in the committed review.md or analysis.md inside it, so a run's two writes are one and a value carried in from before it is none. A second run of a kind is over the bound unless the previous run of that kind left a gating finding: blocking, or a MUST, SHOULD or contract-breaks count, the open fix-round mark, a budget stop or a skipped pass on a review, the last three being runs the gate has re-run, or a hard-fail or blocking-findings count on an analysis. Returns state (measured; shallow-history, which cannot measure; nothing-to-compare, no reopening closed in the window), since, reopenings (feature, start, end, reviews, analyses, over-bound, decisions-by-person, the decisions and waivers stamped with a run's time that a person recorded, so a decline with no reason counts as none, and a decision another spec's record already held, with the same key and outcome, at the parents of the run's first write counts in that record alone, and run-decisions, that count per run), not-counted (feature, start, end, reason: no-recorded-run, record-unparseable or no-in-progress-commit), waiting (in-progress specs at that commit with no unchecked task), and closed-count, over-bound-count, not-counted-count, waiting-count and runs. A walk that could not finish is an error, never a count. When the result would pass [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default), form is `file`, the three lists are absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding them whole. Read-only but for that file.";
pub const MERGE_MANAGED_BLOCK: &str =
    "Idempotently merge a framework-managed block with configurable marker shape.";
pub const MERGE_PERMISSIONS: &str = "Idempotently merge a canonical permission allow/deny set into a JSON file with exact-match dedup. With canonical true it merges Claude's canonical allow, deny and retired sets, which the runtime holds, beside any entries passed, and ensures permissions.additionalDirectories holds the spec root and {config-dir}/commands/{project} as absolute paths, so /{project}:configure passes no entry (spec 080). An allow entry outside Claude's canonical set is refused either way. revoked names each retired entry removed, and directories-added each path appended.";
pub const MERGE_STATUS_HOOK: &str = "Install Claude Code's UserPromptExpansion hooks for /{project}:status and /{project}:help into a settings file (spec 080). It runs `claude --version` first, with a timeout: on 2.1.101 or later it adds, under hooks.UserPromptExpansion, an entry matched to {project}:status whose command is `\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.ductus/bin/ductus\" hook status` and one matched to {project}:help whose command is `\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.ductus/bin/ductus\" hook help`, each naming `ductus.exe` on Windows. Each entry is recognized by its command alone, so one already present is not added again, and every other key and entry is left as it is. action is created when no file existed, updated when either entry was added, and unchanged when both were already there; the whole file is written atomically. On an older version, or one it cannot read, it writes nothing and returns skipped, with reason saying why and claude-version the version read, null when none was. path is the settings file, repo-relative.";
pub const MIGRATE_SESSION_FILE: &str = "Translate a pre-0.10.0 legacy session JSON (`.claude/{project}-session.json`) into the consolidated `.ductus/session.toml`, applying camelCase→kebab-case key renames (`scenarioPath`→`scenario-path`, `setAt`→`set-at`), and delete the legacy file. Idempotent: no-op when no legacy file is present; preserves any existing `.ductus/session.toml`.";
pub const PROCESS_DECISIONS: &str = "Classify the deferred and waived decisions recorded in a spec's review.md or analysis.md against this run's finding keys (matched/lapsed/expired/retained/dedup), before the host gives dispositions, so a settled finding is counted under its stored outcome and not decided again. A firing deferral whose pipeline work is complete — every checkbox of its task checked, a done-when written as one included, its task or tasks.md gone, its spec done or gone, or a rule file — is lapsed: the host passes it to the writer to drop and the finding takes a new disposition; one naming only an external work item never lapses (spec 076). Pass restricted when the run did not evaluate every source: a non-firing decision is then retained, never expired. On the review record a non-firing decision whose key names a file still on disk that the scope of the run's window (since, when it took one) does not hold, a plan's pattern in that scope holding what it matches and a :line, range or :line:column in the key being no part of the file, is retained too, since nothing read that file, as is any naming a file when that scope cannot be read; a key naming no file, and one on a file gone from disk, expire. Each list's count is inline; when the result would pass [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default), form is `file`, no list is inline, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding all five whole, pretty-printed, to read in parts by line offset when it exceeds one read. Read-only but for that file; malformed entries are reported and never pruned. On the CLI, fired is `--fired-json FILE`, read from a staging directory, as apply-manifest's are (BE-INPUT-004), with the JSON the MCP argument takes and its errors.";
pub const PROCESS_WAIVERS: &str = "Classify the waivers recorded in a spec's review.md against currently-firing findings (apply/expire/retain/dedup). Pass skipped-pass for each dimension not run this pass: on a dimension-restricted run a non-firing waiver is retained (kept in frontmatter), never expired, so a partial run cannot prune a waiver anchored to a skipped dimension. A non-firing waiver on a file still on disk that the scope of the run's window (since, when it took one) does not hold, or when that scope cannot be read, is retained too, since nothing read that file. On the CLI, fired is `--fired-json FILE`, read from a staging directory, as apply-manifest's are (BE-INPUT-004), with the JSON the MCP argument takes and its errors.";
pub const PRUNE_PLAN: &str = "Report a feature's plan.md sections outside the design record (the plan template's own ## headings, compared case-insensitively), each with its size, a digest, the plan-record advisory /{project}:analyze records for it, and whether analysis.md stores a person's waiver for that advisory, since a waiver the disposition rule made keeps no section. With apply, remove the listed sections by heading and digest, refusing the whole write when any changed since the preview (stale-sections). On a done spec every call reports whether the tree takes the done → in-progress back-edge against HEAD (reopen-required), by classify-spec-edit's test of an added unchecked line: a preview for the tree as it stands, an apply for the tree it leaves, computed before it writes. With `all`, preview every spec — listing each whose plan holds such a section, or a done spec that already requires a reopen — and refuse `apply`. Never returns a section's text. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: under `all` features and skipped are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const PRUNE_PROJECT: &str = "Segment the four project files (AGENTS.md at the project root, and system.md, events.md and errors.md under the spec root) into entries for /{project}:prune --project (spec 041, for spec 075): each file's state (present, absent, unreadable), bytes and whether it is over [artifacts] read-size-bytes, and each entry's path, enclosing ## heading, kind (heading, item, row, block), line range, bytes and digest, never its text. HTML comments and /ductus-managed BEGIN/END regions are never entries. An events.md or errors.md entry carries its identifier (the event type or error code), and a preview searches the project's tracked files outside the spec root for it literally, listing each hit; with no repository the search is unavailable, never zero hits. With apply, remove or replace the listed entries by path and digest, refusing the whole write when any changed since the preview (stale-entries), and create a missing system.md, events.md or errors.md from its compiled-in template. On the CLI, replace is `--replace-json FILE`, read from a staging directory, as apply-manifest's are (BE-INPUT-004), with the JSON the MCP argument takes and its errors.";
pub const PRUNE_TASKS: &str = "Reduce a feature's tasks.md — drop spent task sections (keep-pending; a suffixed task such as `11a` is a task like any other) or reset to template state; a prune that removes a task section writes the `next-task:` counter in tasks.md frontmatter past the highest number the file held (never lowering one already higher), which append-task honours so a pruned number is never reissued, and a reset keeps the frontmatter, counter included, so a reset file with no counter yields task 1; or, with `all`, every spec's, returning one line per spec (gate, status, counts, sizes, path) and the specs skipped for having no tasks.md. `force` overrides the reset gate on one spec and is refused with `all`. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: sections, for one feature, or features and skipped, under `all`, are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const READ_RE_READ: &str = "Read a /{project}:review fix round's re-read result (spec 078): the result.json the re-read wrote under .ductus/review-rounds/{feature}/{base}/, where `base` is the one the round was briefed at. `state` is `complete` only for a file that parses and says \"complete\": true; `incomplete` for no file or one whose `complete` is not true — a re-read that did not finish, never a clean one; `unparseable` for a file not of the result file's shape (unknown fields, a severity outside MUST/SHOULD/NOTE, a multi-line text, an empty `old`), with `problem` naming why. A complete result counts its findings by severity and counts apart the MUST and SHOULD findings carrying no `edits`, which no diff check can close. A MUST or SHOULD on a path outside the round's round.toml counts as a NOTE, and `demoted` says how many; a complete result whose round.toml is absent or does not parse is `unparseable`. A re-read is clean when it is complete with no MUST and no SHOULD. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`, `findings` is absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding it whole, pretty-printed; `state` and every count stay inline in both forms. Past the read size it also writes .ductus/results/.gitignore and removes the feature's stale list files; read-only otherwise.";
pub const READ_SPEC: &str = "Parse spec frontmatter and body sections. The audit records are not part of the result: read them from the feature's review.md and analysis.md. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: sections, acceptance-criteria, open-questions, scenario-open-questions and scenario-files-unreadable are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const READ_TASKS: &str = "Parse `tasks.md` into a structured task list. A task number is decimal digits and at most one lowercase letter before the dot (`11`, `11a`); `11ab.`, `11A.` and `3a quick wins` are no task. Numbers order by digits, then suffix: `11` before `11a` before `12`. A task's done-when is its Done when line joined, by single spaces, with each following more-indented line that is not a list item, until a blank line, heading, bullet or less-indented line. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: tasks is absent, tasks-count stays inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const RELOCATE_AUDIT_RECORDS: &str = "Move a spec's `review:` and `analyze:` frontmatter blocks into the artifacts that own them (review.md / analysis.md) and remove them from spec.md \u{2014} the sweep behind spec 057's migration. Idempotent: a spec carrying no block is a no-op reporting `changed: false`, so a re-run over a partially migrated corpus completes the remainder. Never invents a record for a spec that has none, never rewrites body prose (the edit is frontmatter-only, so it is a correction under \u{a7}spec-lifecycle and a `done` spec stays `done`), and merges the block into a review.md or analysis.md that already exists rather than overwriting it, the spec block winning where the two disagree, each disagreeing key reported in `disagreements`, since which record is current is a judgement the primitive cannot date.";
pub const REMOVE_INBOX_ITEM: &str = "Remove the first bullet from {specs-root}/inbox.md whose text equals `item` (atomic write). The complement of append-inbox; a no-match or missing inbox is a clean outcome (removed: false). Returns the remaining bullet count.";
pub const RESOLVE_ANCHOR: &str = "Verify `§anchor` references in one file. Markers come from `path` itself by default (self-consistency); pass `markers-path` to resolve one file's references against another file's markers (e.g. a spec's references against the constitution). Each reference has a kind: cross-document (an explicit **§X** in pair into a shipped document, judged by equality against its headings, or a line naming exactly one shipped document), qualified (its line names another document, or a code span holding a path and the reference: judged against the headings and markers of every readable document named, a registered [constitutions.*] document among them, the citing file's headings and the markers), intra-document (a heading of the citing file) or markers. The markers file is recognized by where a path lands, from the citing file's directory or the root, never by its basename. A reference it cannot judge is not unresolved: unjudged carries the reason, anchor-target-unjudgeable when every document named is absent or one is not markdown, anchor-target-unreadable when one exists unreadable or is a registered entry that did not resolve, each with its count. A registry that does not parse halts the run.";
pub const RESOLVE_CONSTITUTIONS: &str = "Resolve the project config's [constitutions] registry (spec 055) to shared-constitution documents on local disk; a malformed entry halts, naming the alias and field. For each registered alias, resolve its local `path` and look for `constitution.md` inside it; the `repo` URL is identity only and is never fetched, so there is no transport and no network failure mode. Returns `loaded` and `skipped` separately — a missing checkout is `not-checked-out` and a checkout holding no document is `no-constitution-document`, two distinct operator mistakes with distinct messages. Empty `loaded` with empty `skipped` means none registered; empty `loaded` with a non-empty `skipped` means registered and unreadable, and the caller must not collapse them. Aliases sharing a path are reported in `duplicate-paths` (warn and allow; the document is read once). When any entry is registered, each `loaded` record carries its document's `bytes` and `over-limit` against the result's `threshold` (`[artifacts] read-size-bytes`, 50,000 by default; a document at it is not over), plus a `warning` naming the alias, size, limit and owning `repo` as the fix when over; a loaded record without `bytes` and every `skipped` record were not measured, never under the limit. A malformed read size halts the call only when an entry is registered; with none, the result carries no `threshold`. `unmeasured: true` resolves without measuring and reads no `[artifacts]` setting, as `/ductus` calls it. `examined` counts the registered entries, 0 when none.";
pub const RESOLVE_FEATURE: &str = "Resolve an identifier (exact directory name, feature number like 7/007, or partial slug substring) to a feature directory under the configured spec root. Returns the directory name, repo-relative path, spec status, and — when a scenario slug is supplied — the scenario file's existence plus its `section` frontmatter. Ambiguity returns the sorted candidate list and no-match returns not-found, both as domain outcomes for the host to mediate.";
pub const RESOLVE_REFERENCES: &str = "Resolve a consumer feature's `references:` index against the config's [services] registry (`.ductus/config.toml`, with the `.govern/` and legacy-root fallbacks): for each cross-service reference, read the linked spec's live lifecycle status from its local checkout and classify the outcome (ok / unregistered / not-checked-out / broken / status-unreadable). Also returns duplicate-repos, each repo the registry holds under two or more aliases, compared with a trailing slash and `.git` removed, with those aliases, sorted, and empty when none: a link to such a repo resolves through one alias silently. A config that does not parse, or an entry whose `repo` or `path` is absent or empty, halts naming the file, and the entry's alias and field. When the result would pass [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default), form is `file`, both lists are absent, and lists-file names a JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding them whole; `references-count` and `duplicate-repos-count` are inline in both forms.";
pub const RESOLVE_SESSION: &str = "Resolve this process's session target (spec 062). The identity comes from the runtime's own environment: DUCTUS_SESSION, then the agent's platform session id (Claude Code: CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID), else none. An unidentified process reads the shared default (.ductus/session.toml). An identified one gets its own target (.ductus/sessions/{key}.toml), or adopts and pins the shared default when it has none, or no target when it was cleared. Returns identity, source (own / adopted / default / cleared / none), target, notices to display once (adoption, other sessions on the same feature, a fold or consolidation that moved the target), and unreadable: session files that did not parse, so were not checked for the same feature. Commands call this instead of reading the session file by hand. `identity` is null when the process is unidentified; `target` is null for `cleared` and `none`; each notice is `{kind, message}`, kind one of `adopted`, `co-target`, `folded` or `consolidated`, and message the sentence to display; `unreadable` is omitted when empty (`dashboard` carries it as `session-unreadable`).";
pub const RETARGET_SESSIONS: &str = "After /{project}:fold or /{project}:consolidate removes a feature directory, re-point or clear every session in the working tree that names it (spec 062): the shared default (.ductus/session.toml) and every per-process target (.ductus/sessions/*.toml). `from` (the removed feature) and `cause` (`fold` or `consolidate`) are required; `feature` and `path` give the new target, with optional `scenario` and `scenario-path` together, or `clear` takes the place of a target, and giving both is an invalid argument. cause=fold with the upstream feature+path (optional scenario) re-targets; cause=consolidate with clear clears. Sessions naming other features are untouched; every affected session other than the caller's gets a notice its next resolve-session delivers. Returns retargeted and cleared session labels, `default` standing for the shared default, and unreadable files left in place, since a removal cannot prove they do not name `from`.";
pub const RETIRE_FEATURE: &str = "Remove a branch-scoped feature directory (`<identifier>.<n>-<slug>`) once its content has been folded into the upstream spec that receives it — the last step of a fold and the only irreversible one. This is where the fold target's existence is finally enforced: everything earlier treats an absent target as the normal case — validation checks `folds-into` for shape only, and the pre-done gate blocks on a fold only when its target is in this tree — because a branch-scoped spec exists precisely because upstream diverged, so before the merge its target normally lives on the upstream branch. Fold-back runs after the merge, in the first tree holding both, which is the first moment the question has an answer worth refusing on. Refuses when `fold-target` names no feature directory holding a spec.md (a directory with no spec is not a home content can have landed in), and refuses a sequential `NNN-slug` feature unless `allow-sequential` is set — the sequential form is permanent, and the refusal is gated rather than removed so that an irreversible operation stays out of reach of a typo (even with the flag, a `feature` that is not a feature directory name — `.`, a dotfile, a name with no number — is refused before anything touches the filesystem): fold never sets the flag, so a mistyped feature name on a fold still refuses, while consolidation sets it having already named both specs and confirmed the removal. The spec.md guard above is **not** gated — opting in to removing a sequential directory is not opting in to stranding its content. `retired: false` is the domain outcome for a directory already gone, so re-running an interrupted fold converges rather than halting.";
pub const REWRITE_SPEC_LINKS: &str = "Re-point every inbound pointer to a retiring or renamed feature directory at its fold target: markdown body links across the spec corpus, the `folds-into` frontmatter field, and the spec-root paths named in the referrers `check-orphaned-references` reads outside it (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `.githooks/pre-commit`). The frontmatter key is included deliberately — it is the one pointer whose job is to survive until the merge, so a rename that repaired body links and left it behind would break exactly the thing the field exists for. `dependencies:` and `references:` are NOT touched: they are derived from body links, and derive-dependencies / derive-references regenerate them from the rewritten bodies on the next commit. Matching is by whole path segment, so a directory whose name merely shares a prefix is left alone, and cross-service URLs are never rewritten. `to` is `<feature>` (each link's tail survives; one naming a file the fold did not write is listed in `kept-tails`) or `<feature>/<scenario>` (a fold into one scenario — a `scenarios/{name}` tail is kept with its fragment, and every other tail becomes the routed scenario). A scenario tail the retiring directory does not hold is listed in `kept-tails` on either target. A link inside the target's own directory that climbs to the spec root is written relative to its file (`scenarios/x.md`, `../spec.md`), never through `../<target>/`, which derive-dependencies would record as a self-cycle. `mentions` lists each line of an examined file that still names the retiring directory, by its name or the number it is cited by, outside the derived `dependencies:` and `references:`. Both halves of `to` are held to the feature-directory and scenario-slug grammar. Every referrer, and every markdown file under the spec root, is read before anything is written: a referrer that cannot be rewritten safely (a symbolic link, a parent resolving outside the project, text that is not UTF-8) is named in `skipped`, as is any other symbolic link under the spec root named `*.md` or to a directory, which is not followed (a spec root that is itself a link is followed), and any other spec-root file that cannot be read as UTF-8 text, or a directory that cannot be listed, stops the run. Empty `rewritten` means examined-and-nothing-pointed-here only when `examined` is non-zero and `skipped` is empty. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: rewritten, skipped, kept-tails and mentions are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const RUN_GENERATOR: &str = "Invoke a bash generator with `--dry-run`; non-zero exit is drift. Not an MCP tool: run `ductus run-generator`, since an MCP call without a prompt would run a command the project supplies (§runtime-boundary).";
pub const SET_STATUS: &str = "Update the `status:` field in spec frontmatter, guarded by `from`. A stale `from` fails loudly, so read the frontmatter before retrying.";
pub const START_FIX_ROUNDS: &str = "Start a /{project}:review run's fix rounds (spec 078): clear the feature's earlier rounds under .ductus/review-rounds/{feature}/ — a directory that ignores itself — and snapshot the whole working tree as the first round's base. The snapshot is a commit object holding every tracked file as it is on disk, every untracked file that is not ignored, and no deleted one, with HEAD as its parent; it moves no ref, writes no index file and changes no working-tree file. `files` counts what it holds, and `budget` is the re-read budget: after that many re-reads that found a MUST or SHOULD, start no round without the operator's decision. Call it when the passes end and before any fix; an error is never a base, so the run's first round is then recorded as not re-read.";
pub const TRAVERSE_DEPS: &str = "Traverse spec dependencies and check status compatibility. Past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`: dependencies and cycles are absent, each with its -count inline, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding the moved lists whole.";
pub const VALIDATE_FRONTMATTER: &str = "Validate an artifact's frontmatter against the row of the pipeline schema its path names (spec 067). A spec.md takes the spec row (status and dependencies required, folds-into and cross-spec-impact checked for shape, a residual review: or analyze: block reported), and its run also checks the review.md and analysis.md beside it (one that exists but will not load, or whose decisions: list will not parse, is a hard fail; an absent one is no finding) and every scenario under its scenarios/ directory, each finding keyed by its scenarios/<file> path, so one call covers the whole feature. A .md file under a scenarios/ directory, whatever its name, takes the scenario row: section, or the legacy spec-ref, must be a non-empty string, and a missing status or dependencies is no finding. A review.md or analysis.md takes that record's checks alone, and an absent one is refused. Any other path is refused, never checked against a row that does not describe it. A YAML parse failure, or a scenario beside a spec that cannot be read or has no closed fence, is a hard-fail finding; a named file that cannot be read or has no closed fence is an error.";
pub const WRITE_ANALYSIS: &str = "Record that /{project}:analyze ran, by writing specs/NNN/analysis.md — the record in its frontmatter, the findings in a fixed body skeleton. It refuses while the spec's tasks.md holds an unchecked box under any heading but a task this run's own deferral names, since an analysis records once implementation is complete (spec 079). The durable counterpart to review.md, and what `check-review-gate` reads to hold a spec out of `done` until the second gate has actually run. Takes the run's hard-fail / blocking / advisory counts (from the re-check after the fix-and-route step) plus `unexamined` (the skipped-target count, so a clean record cannot be read as a fully-examined one), and `findings`, each with its tier and disposition. Records the two gating tiers net of the findings released by a deferral naming pipeline work on another spec still to do, sets `blocking` from them, and derives a dispositions: map in which a live finding the caller did not itemize counts as undispositioned; refuses a waiver on a hard-fail or blocking finding; stores deferred and waived decisions under decisions: (decided-by required, but for what the disposition rule decided, stored as decided-by: rule; a person's stored decision for the same key wins over the rule's in the counts and the body as in the record, unless this run expired it), marking a gating deferral that releases nothing gating: true, and prunes expired-decisions (spec 076). Nothing is written to the inbox. Records the rule assessments (spec 082): from the rule files it discovers itself (every file in the rule-file directory) it derives each assessable rule the run neither judged (`judged-rules`, or a `rule-verdicts` key) nor reused from the prior record, whose verdict is current while the design subjects (spec, plan, data model, scenarios) and the rule file are unchanged, and records them under `unjudged-rules`, counted into `rule-assessments-not-checked`; stores `rule-verdicts` (a fired rule's `addressed` verdict with a `cite`, `<path>#<Heading>` of a design subject, which must resolve, else the rule is recorded unjudged, or its `finding` with the `tier` it was counted in, `must` or `should`, and `at` and `message`), carries forward each current prior verdict the run did not judge, and records a digest per rule file under `rule-digest`; refuses a verdict naming no loaded rule, a `finding` whose `tier` differs from its rule\'s Statement unless the Statement carries both MUST and SHOULD, and a tier count below the number of `finding` verdicts that stored that tier; and returns `contradictions`, each rule judged a finding over unchanged subjects after a not-fired or addressed verdict, which hold nothing. Refuses a spec whose frontmatter does not parse rather than recording a clean run into it. Atomic. On the CLI, findings, expired-decisions, decided-by and rule-verdicts are `--findings-json`, `--expired-decisions-json`, `--decided-by-json` and `--rule-verdicts-json`, each a FILE read from a staging directory, as apply-manifest's are (BE-INPUT-004), with the JSON the MCP argument takes and its errors, and judged-rules is `--judged-rule`, repeated.";
pub const WRITE_REVIEW: &str = "Render specs/NNN/review.md — the review report and, in that same file's frontmatter, the record of the run. It refuses while the spec's tasks.md holds an unchecked box under any heading but a task this run's own deferral names, since a review records once implementation is complete (spec 079). `spec.md` is not written: each audit record has one home (spec 057). Consumes the pass findings as a single array (plus waiver results, scope scalars, and skipped-pass flags), applies the cross-pass dedup (highest-severity-wins on rule-id + file + overlapping range) before counting, buckets survivors into MUST / SHOULD / low-confidence / waived / deferred — a violation leaves the counts when its deferral names pipeline work on another spec still to do, which is what releases it (spec 076) — prunes expired waivers, records each new-waivers item as one waivers: entry listing every file its rule fires at this run that no waiver holds, stamped from reviewed-at and decided-by, applying an anchor this run's earlier write stored without storing it again and refusing one with nothing to waive unless that write stored it, so a run's two writes carry one set of arguments and a fix between them may clear the finding (spec 079), and emits the 0-findings / blocking:false report for empty scope, naming a break it holds. It refuses a diff-base no scope can be derived from; an empty one, a spec never in-progress, records scope 0 (spec 079). Each observation carries its disposition (fixed, deferred with its work, waived with its reason, or undispositioned by default), by-rule when the disposition rule decided it, and contract-break when it shows the spec's own code breaks its contract, which the rule never waives and which counts in contract-breaks unless fixed, a waived one refused while the rule's stored waiver for its key stands outside expired-decisions (spec 079), and a violation may carry a deferral; open-findings lists each counted violation's file and each broken contract's that the run did not fix, a person did not waive and no deferral releases, which must then name its path, and a write whose scope is empty, by the flag or by a window holding nothing but the run's own record, refuses over an earlier record counting a finding or one it cannot read (spec 079); the record counts the observations in a derived dispositions: map, a newly deferred or waived observation, or newly deferred violation, is stored under decisions:, stamped decided-by: rule when by-rule and otherwise by decided-by, which is then required, a person's stored decision winning over the rule, a deferral that releases nothing is marked gating: true, a decision on a broken contract is marked contract-break: true, so a later run matching it counts and lists the break, with no host marking it or naming its file again, unless the decision is in expired-decisions (spec 079), and expired-decisions from process-decisions are pruned. A fix-rounds list, one entry per fix round with its check, derives the record's fix-rounds, re-reads, diff-checked, not-re-read, budget-stop and round-waivers, waiving an un-re-read round with its own reason (decided-by required) or the [review] re-reads-off reason; without the list none of them is written (spec 078). loop-open writes fix-rounds-open: true, which every review-command write but the last passes (spec 079). The record's excluded count and Excluded from scope section name each path the scope left out, by the spec that claimed it, and each path a round's excluded list left out, by the round. Nothing is written to the inbox. Atomic. On the CLI, findings, applied-waivers, expired-waivers, new-waivers, observations, expired-decisions, decided-by and fix-rounds are each `--{name}-json FILE` read from a staging directory, as apply-manifest's are (BE-INPUT-004), with the JSON the MCP argument takes and its errors; a fix-rounds file holding [] is the empty list, and no flag leaves it absent. The result's analyze-freshness names the analysis record's state against the working tree; past [artifacts] read-size-bytes (50,000 by default) form is `file`, a stale state carries no paths key, analyze-stale-paths-count says how many, and lists-file names the JSON file under .ductus/results/ holding them whole under analyze-stale-paths.";
pub const WRITE_SESSION: &str = "Atomically merge-write the active session file (`.ductus/session.toml`, falling back to `.govern/session.toml` then the legacy root `.govern.session.toml` pre-migration; gitignored). A target write (supply `feature`+`path`, optional `scenario`) sets the target and preserves the per-contributor `cli-config-dir`; a host-config write (supply only `cli-config-dir`) sets the agent config-dir and preserves the existing target. Since spec 062 an identified process (DUCTUS_SESSION, or a platform session id such as CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID) also writes its own target under .ductus/sessions/, and the result reports other sessions on the same feature (peers), the per-process targets its expiry sweep removed (expired), session files it could not read (unreadable), and any pending removal notice the write replaced (notices). Pairs with `resolve-session`'s read; allowing this MCP tool once suppresses the per-invocation Write permission prompt the host-write path triggers. The result adds `identity` and `own-path` (the per-process file written), both absent when unidentified, so an unidentified write keeps its earlier shape. Each peer is `{session, feature, scenario, last-used}`: `scenario` null when the peer targets none, `last-used` the peer record's used-at as an RFC 3339 UTC time to the second with `Z`, or null when it holds none; peers are empty on a clear, a host-config write and an unidentified write. `unreadable` includes this process's own record and the shared default when either does not parse, which this write replaces (a replaced default's `cli-config-dir` is not carried forward). The four lists are omitted when empty. `clear` removes the target block (feature, path, scenario, scenario-path, set-at) and keeps `cli-config-dir`; it excludes a target write, and a `cli-config-dir` supplied with it still applies.";

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::summary;

    #[test]
    fn a_summary_ends_at_the_first_sentence() {
        assert_eq!(summary("Do this. Then that. And more."), "Do this.");
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_summary_ends_at_a_colon_that_comes_first() {
        assert_eq!(
            summary("Run the checks: one, two. Then more."),
            "Run the checks"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_summary_ends_at_a_colon_with_no_sentence_end() {
        assert_eq!(summary("Run the checks: one, two."), "Run the checks");
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_sentence_ending_before_a_later_colon_is_the_summary() {
        assert_eq!(summary("Do this. It returns: a."), "Do this.");
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_dot_or_colon_inside_a_word_ends_nothing() {
        assert_eq!(
            summary("Write .ductus/session.toml for /ductus:target."),
            "Write .ductus/session.toml for /ductus:target."
        );
    }
}
